Shrink OpenBLAS' RISCV64_GENERIC GEMM memory footprint so f32 neural-network
training fits on a ~28 MB device (e.g. the Milk-V Duo).

OpenBLAS lazily allocates a per-GEMM scratch buffer (BUFFER_SIZE, 32 MB on
riscv64) sized for its default N-block (SGEMM_DEFAULT_R = 12288).  That single
32 MB allocation does not fit on the device, so the first f32 matrix-multiply
is OOM-killed at startup. Our matrices are tiny (e.g. 64 x 297), so shrinking
the N-block to 2048 and the buffer to 8 MB costs no measurable speed.
See the example README / BINARY_SIZE.md and the
movement-recognition tutorial for the full investigation.

Applied with `patch -p1` against an OpenBLAS source tree (tested with 0.3.29).
--- a/common_riscv64.h
+++ b/common_riscv64.h
@@ -86,7 +86,7 @@



-#define BUFFER_SIZE     ( 32 << 20)
+#define BUFFER_SIZE     (  8 << 20)
 #define SEEK_ADDRESS

 #if defined(C910V) || defined(RISCV64_ZVL256B) || defined(RISCV64_ZVL128B) || defined(x280)
--- a/param.h
+++ b/param.h
@@ -3040,10 +3040,10 @@
 #define CGEMM_DEFAULT_Q 120
 #define ZGEMM_DEFAULT_Q 120

-#define SGEMM_DEFAULT_R 12288
-#define DGEMM_DEFAULT_R 8192
-#define CGEMM_DEFAULT_R 4096
-#define ZGEMM_DEFAULT_R 4096
+#define SGEMM_DEFAULT_R 2048
+#define DGEMM_DEFAULT_R 2048
+#define CGEMM_DEFAULT_R 2048
+#define ZGEMM_DEFAULT_R 2048

 #define SYMV_P	16
